body:
Users wrapping `ReturnFile*` or `Redirect*` calls in try-catch blocks inadvertently swallow `DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException`, breaking request flow control.

## Changes

- **New analyzer (DotVVM06)**: Detects when `ReturnFile*` or `RedirectTo*` methods on `IDotvvmRequestContext` are called within try-catch blocks that catch `DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException` (or base types like `Exception`) without rethrowing
- **Flexible rethrow detection**: Allows `throw;` anywhere in catch block, including inside if statements or other nested operations
- **When clause support**: Recognizes when clauses that exclude the interrupt exception (e.g., `when (ex is not DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException)`)
- **Nested try block validation**: Validates all enclosing try blocks to ensure the exception properly propagates through nested try-catch structures
- **Bare catch block handling**: Bare `catch { }` blocks (without exception type) are always flagged as problematic since we cannot reliably detect rethrows within them. Users should use explicit exception types instead.
- **Resource strings**: Added localized diagnostic messages
- **Tests**: 20 test cases covering various redirect/return file methods, nested try-catch, when clauses, bare catch blocks, and edge cases

## Example

The analyzer flags these patterns:

```csharp
// Catching base exception without rethrow
try
{
    await context.ReturnFileAsync(bytes, "file.csv", "application/octet-stream");
}
catch (Exception ex)  // DotVVM06: DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException must be rethrown
{
    // Handle exceptions
}

// Bare catch block (always flagged)
try
{
    await context.ReturnFileAsync(bytes, "file.csv", "application/octet-stream");
}
catch  // DotVVM06: DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException must be rethrown
{
    throw;  // Even with rethrow, bare catch is flagged
}
```

And accepts these patterns:

```csharp
// Rethrow in dedicated catch block
try
{
    await context.ReturnFileAsync(bytes, "file.csv", "application/octet-stream");
}
catch (DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException)
{
    throw;
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    // Handle other exceptions
}

// Rethrow inside if statement
try
{
    await context.ReturnFileAsync(bytes, "file.csv", "application/octet-stream");
}
catch (DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException ex)
{
    if (someCondition)
    {
        throw;
    }
}

// Exclude using when clause
try
{
    await context.ReturnFileAsync(bytes, "file.csv", "application/octet-stream");
}
catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException)
{
    // Handle other exceptions only
}
```

> [!WARNING]
>
><issue_title>Roslyn analyzer to detect catching DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException</issue_title>
><issue_description>A couple of users were dealing with this unintuitive behavior - when they perform a redirect or returning a file, they wrap it in a try/catch block, and catch all exceptions. However, DotVVM throws `DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException` which needs to be rethrown.
> 
> ```csharp
> public async Task OnDownloadFile()
> {
>   try
>   {
>      ...
>      await Context.ReturnFileAsync(bytes, "file.csv", "application/octet-stream");
>   }
>   catch (System.Exception ex)
>   {
>     ...
>   }
> }
> ```
> 
> We already have Roslyn analyzers in the project. We should detect this pattern when `ReturnFile*` or `Redirect*` is called on `IDotvvmRequestContext`, and if it is inside try/catch block, ensure there is a special case for `DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException` where the exception is rethrown as such:
> 
> ```
>   catch (DotvvmInterruptRequestExecutionException) 
>   {
>     throw;
>   }
> ```</issue_description>
> 
><agent_instructions>There is already a project with analyzers - implement it there. 
> There is also a project with tests for the analyzers - make sure that the functionality is covered by tests.</agent_instructions>
> 
> ## Comments on the Issue (you are @copilot in this section)
> 
><comments>
></comments>
>

- Fixes riganti/dotvvm#1964

