These 'if' statements can be combined.

---

In general, to fix this type of issue you replace a nested <code>if</code> whose body is only another <code>if</code> (and no <code>else</code> branches) with one <code>if</code> statement whose condition is the conjunction (<code>&amp;&amp;</code>) of the original two conditions. This preserves behavior while simplifying control flow.</p>
<p>In this file, inside <code>HasProperExceptionHandling</code>, we have:</p>
<pre><code>if (current is ITryOperation tryOperation)
{
    if (!HasProperRethrow(tryOperation, dotvvmInterruptException))
        return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>We should replace it with:</p>
<pre><code>if (current is ITryOperation tryOperation &amp;&amp;
    !HasProperRethrow(tryOperation, dotvvmInterruptException))
{
    return false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps the pattern matching (<code>tryOperation</code>) and the <code>HasProperRethrow</code> call exactly the same, only moving the second condition into the <code>if</code> header. No new imports or helpers are required, and no other lines in the file need modification.","body_updated_after_merge":false,"commit_id":"51c7db2e0625e9c7c8735652b5bdb370ed574606","created_at":"2026-02-15T11:39:12Z","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,191 @@
+using System.Collections.Immutable;
