- [x] Remove `BaseContinuation.recursing` field
- [x] Embed state machine in `invokeResume()` for all coroutine kinds
- [x] Fix `super.X()` calls inside coroutine thunks via helper methods
- [x] Add `testRecursiveAfterSuspension` and `testOverridingCoroutine` regression tests
- [x] Fix `cf_captured` type initialization to use `inside.result_type`
- [x] Fix `WUnboundTypeParameter` warnings in invokeResume HXB serialisation
- [x] Fix `libneko.so.2` not found for `haxelib`
- [x] Fix `WUnboundTypeParameter` warnings — 0 warnings vs `development` baseline
- [x] Fix if-statement dropped from single-state coroutines
- [x] Fix type parameters at creation time: `vcontinuation` typed correctly
- [x] Pass hoisted args to constructor for both inline and thunk paths
- [x] Remove pointless spill of unused function args in state 0
- [x] Move thunk closure to last constructor arg: both modes now use `(completion, arg1, ..., argN)` with thunk appended as last arg — `new HxCoro(completion, arg1, arg2)` (inline) vs `new HxCoro(completion, arg1, arg2, thunk)` (thunk)

<!-- START COPILOT ORIGINAL PROMPT -->



<details>

<summary>Original prompt</summary>

> 
> ----
> 
> *This section details on the original issue you should resolve*
> 
> <issue_title>[coro] The `BaseContinuation.recursing` situation</issue_title>
> <issue_description>I wanted to check this again because we basically blindly inherited this from Kotlin and I still find it a little awkward. I don't actually want to talk too much about how I _think_ it works so that I don't cause any wrong assumptions, so I instead present how to observe the problem:
> 
> If in coro.ml we remove the code that sets `_hx_continuation.recursing = true` in the coroutine body (lines 347-353), this example errors:
> 
> ```haxe
> import haxe.Exception;
> import haxe.coro.IContinuation;
> import haxe.coro.context.Context;
> 
> class MyContinuation<T> implements IContinuation<T> {
> 	public var context(get, null):Context;
> 
> 	public function new(context:Context) {
> 		this.context = context;
> 	}
> 
> 	public function get_context() {
> 		return context;
> 	}
> 
> 	public function resume(result:T, error:Exception) {
> 		trace(result, error);
> 	}
> }
> 
> function main() {
> 	@:coroutine function yield() { }
> 	var maxIters = 3;
> 	var counter = 0;
> 
> 	@:coroutine function foo() {
> 		if (++counter < maxIters) {
> 			yield();
> 			foo();
> 		}
> 	}
> 
> 	final cont = new MyContinuation(Context.create());
> 	trace(foo(cont).error); // Invalid coroutine state
> }
> ```
> 
> While I think I understand why, it bothers me that we have this cruft at the entry-point of every coroutine function that doesn't get TCO-optimized:
> 
> ```js
> 		let _hx_continuation;
> 		if(((_hx_completion) instanceof _$Main_HxCoro_$_$Main_$Main_$Fields_$_$AnonFunc1) && _hx_completion.recursing == false) {
> 			_hx_continuation = _hx_completion;
> 		} else {
> 			_hx_continuation = new _$Main_HxCoro_$_$Main_$Main_$Fields_$_$AnonFunc1(foo,_hx_completion);
> 		}
> ```
> 
> Having an `is` check at such a central location feels wrong and is why I think we should evaluate this carefully and see if there's a better alternative for Haxe.
> 
> @Copilot Please take a look and evaluate the problem. There's a chance that no better solution exists, but let me know if you come up with any other solutions!</issue_description>
> 
> ## Comments on the Issue (you are @copilot in this section)
> 
> <comments>
> </comments>
> 


</details>



<!-- START COPILOT CODING AGENT SUFFIX -->

- Fixes HaxeFoundation/haxe#12644

