Your version would add two helper functions for `Parent.test` from the two `super.test()` calls in `Child`. I don't see why it should do that, so instead of having a `Hashtbl` I've made it look up the intended field name in `curclass.cl_fields`. Please confirm that I didn't miss anything with this change!", "body_updated_after_merge": null, "date_basis": "created_at", "event_at": "2026-02-21T21:36:55Z", "event_key": "pr_comments:3972043615:7c9558357cabaa3c520a449a175924becd715d30213c12df42d45a19f6d13441:1", "parent_review_event_key": null, "request_at": "2026-02-21T14:14:53Z", "request_event_key": "pr_timeline:3972043615:d8d077e2836eb7983250ec768b343020c24e4f846c779c465cda9440f6462273:1", "review_state": null, "role_proxy": "non_author_user", "source_event_at": "2026-02-21T21:36:55Z", "source_table": "pr_comments", "source_url": "https://github.com/HaxeFoundation/haxe/pull/12645#issuecomment-3939545935"}, {"actor": "Simn", "body": "@copilot I have pushed a commit where the idea is to accurately track during the initial transformation if our current coroutine captures any outside variables or accesses `this` or `super`. I'm pretty sure that in such cases we can basically treat the coroutine like a static function and inline its code in `invokeResume`.

