🟡 **normalizeSecretName truncation can produce trailing hyphens, resulting in invalid Azure Key Vault names**

The `normalizeSecretName` method removes trailing hyphens at line 63, but the truncation to 127 characters at line 72 happens *after* that step. If truncation cuts the string at a position where the last character is a hyphen (e.g., a long path like `/a/very/.../long-name` normalizing to `a-very-...-long-name` and truncating to end at a `-`), the result will have a trailing hyphen, which is invalid per Azure Key Vault naming rules. The same issue applies if the `secret-` prefix at line 67 pushes the string past 127 characters — the truncated result could end with a hyphen.

```suggestion
    // Truncate to 127 characters
    if (normalized.length > 127) {
      normalized = normalized.substring(0, 127).replace(/-+$/, '');
    }
```

