🔴 **Error wrapping in AwsSsmSecretProvider.setSecret breaks the push throttling retry mechanism**

The new try/catch in `setSecret` wraps all AWS SDK errors — including throttling errors like `TooManyUpdates`, `ThrottlingException`, and `TooManyRequestsException` — into a `SecretOperationError` (whose `.name` is `'SecretOperationError'`). The `PushEnvToSsmCommandHandler.retryWithBackoff` at `src/envilder/application/pushEnvToSsm/PushEnvToSsmCommandHandler.ts:178-184` checks `error.name` against those specific AWS error names to decide whether to retry. Because the original error name is now lost inside the `SecretOperationError` wrapper, the retry logic will never trigger — all throttling errors will immediately fail instead of retrying with exponential backoff.

<details><summary>Retry logic that no longer matches</summary>

```typescript
// PushEnvToSsmCommandHandler.ts:178-184
const isThrottlingError =
  typeof error === 'object' &&
  error !== null &&
  'name' in error &&
  (error.name === 'TooManyUpdates' ||        // never matches
    error.name === 'ThrottlingException' ||   // never matches
    error.name === 'TooManyRequestsException'); // never matches
// error.name is now always 'SecretOperationError'
```

</details>

