[{"author_association":null,"body":"The Base64 input is ASCII; using UTF8 here is unnecessary and slightly obscures what’s being tested. Consider using `Text.Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(...)` (consistent with the rest of this file) and avoid the extra `"Hello World"u8.ToArray()` allocation by using the span-based `Convert.ToBase64String(ReadOnlySpan<byte>)` overload (or a literal base64 string) to keep the test lean and consistent.","body_updated_after_merge":false,"commit_id":"5afe5e718ebf6f199e799ad4d0dbc92417914e2c","created_at":"2026-02-16T22:47:11Z","diff_hunk":"@@ -439,5 +439,36 @@ public void TransformBlock_OutputBufferTooSmall_ThrowsArgumentOutOfRangeExceptio
             AssertExtensions.Throws<ArgumentOutOfRangeException>("outputBuffer",
                 () => transform.TransformBlock(block, 0, block.Length, destination, 0));
         }
+
+        [Theory]
+        [InlineData(0)]
+        [InlineData(1)]
+        public void TransformFinalBlock_ShouldResetStateConsistently(int finalBlockSize)
+        {
+            using FromBase64Transform transform = new();
+            byte[] destination = new byte[2048];
+            byte[] partialBlock = [(byte)'A'];
+            byte[] finalBlock = new byte[finalBlockSize];
+            finalBlock.AsSpan().Fill((byte)'A');
+
+            // First, do a TransformBlock with a partial block of one unit
+            transform.TransformBlock(partialBlock, 0, partialBlock.Length, destination, 0);
+
+            // Call TransformFinalBlock with either zero or one input
+            // This still results in a partial block (we need four for a complete block)
+            byte[] transformed = transform.TransformFinalBlock(finalBlock, 0, finalBlockSize);
+
+            // The transform should return an empty buffer for partial blocks
+            Assert.Empty(transformed);
+
+            // Now try to reuse the transform with a complete block
+            // This should succeed without observing leftover data from before
+            byte[] complete = Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(Convert.ToBase64String("Hello World"u8.ToArray()));
+            transformed = transform.TransformFinalBlock(complete, 0, complete.Length);
+
+            // Verify we get the expected output
+            string result = Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetString(transformed);
+            Assert.Equal("Hello World", result);","duplicate_id_conflict":false,"event_at":"2026-02-16T22:47:11+00:00","event_key":"inline_comment:2814281266","event_type":"inline_comment","html_url":null,"id":"2814281266","in_reply_to_id":null,"is_feedback_candidate":false,"is_human":false,"line":null,"original_commit_id":"2bb556b031e6ee74a4e581a3762a96876008411c","original_line":null,"original_position":33,"original_start_line":null,"path":"src/libraries/System.Security.Cryptography/tests/Base64TransformsTests.cs","position":33,"pull_request_review_id":"3810800706","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/dotnet/runtime/pulls/124480","side":null,"source_table":"pr_review_comments","source_url":"https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124480","start_line":null,"start_side":null,"state":null,"submitted_at":null,"temporal_relation":"during_pr","updated_at":"2026-02-16T22:47:11Z","url":null,"user":"Copilot","user_id":null,"user_type":"Bot"}]

[{"author_association":null,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Sync runtime version metadata with v1.0.1 bump**

This commit updates source/header banners to `v1.0.1` (e.g. here) but does not update `src/OTGW-firmware/version.h`, which still defines `_SEMVER_FULL` as `1.1.0-beta+eb4319e`; because `sendDeviceInfo()` and `sendMQTTversioninfo()` publish `_SEMVER_FULL`, devices built from this commit will continue reporting themselves as `1.1.0-beta`, causing incorrect version reporting in REST/MQTT clients and any tooling that relies on those values for release tracking.

Useful? React with 👍 / 👎.","body_updated_after_merge":true,"commit_id":"c04d1942f0561539e2454f35ebc437388dba5cbd","created_at":"2026-02-15T18:55:07Z","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
